Certainly! The CWD (Change Working Directory) command is typically used to change the current directory on the server, and the NOOP (No Operation) command is used to keep the connection alive without performing any action.

Here’s how you could modify your sequence of client requests to include the CWD and NOOP commands in appropriate locations:

```
USER ubuntu
PASS ubuntu
SYST
PWD
CWD /path/to/directory  # Change this to the desired directory path
NOOP                    # Keep the connection alive
PORT 127,0,0,1,132,209
LIST
MKD test
QUIT
```

In this modified sequence:
- The CWD command is added after the PWD command to change the working directory to the specified path.
- The NOOP command is added after the CWD command to keep the connection alive, though it is optional and can be omitted depending on the server's requirements. 

Make sure to replace `/path/to/directory` with the actual directory you want to change to on the server. If you don't need to change directories, you can remove the CWD command

